Date: Sun, 24 Aug 2026 01:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 37/37] fat: a cache the size of the machine, not of 1996

64 blocks of 512 bytes is 32 KB. That is the whole data cache the FAT
handler gets, on a machine with 840 MB of RAM.

Measured under ISSUE-0045: opening a 10 MB library makes 6844 scattered
reads, most of them a few dozen bytes, because the file carries thousands of
ELF sections. Every one of them misses a 32 KB cache, and every miss costs a
full multi-block transfer -- the card driver's own counters report 8 KB moved
per command and 20 MB read for a 10 MB file, still climbing when the run was
cut. The card is not slow and the driver is not stuck: the same data is being
fetched over and over because nothing holds it.

2048 blocks is 1 MB, with the hash grown to match so a lookup stays four
blocks deep.

This is a size, not a policy. A working set larger than the cache still
thrashes; what changes is that an ordinary file stops being larger than it.
---
--- a/rom/filesys/fat/volume.c
+++ b/rom/filesys/fat/volume.c
@@ -185,7 +185,26 @@
         return ERROR_UNKNOWN;
     }
 
-    sb->cache = Cache_CreateCache(glob, 64, 64, sb->sectorsize, SysBase,
+    /*
+     * 64 blocks of 512 bytes is 32 KB, and that is not a cache for this
+     * machine's working set.
+     *
+     * Measured under ISSUE-0045: opening a 10 MB library makes 6844 scattered
+     * reads, most of them a few dozen bytes, because the file has thousands of
+     * ELF sections. With a 32 KB cache every one of them misses, and each miss
+     * costs a full multi-block transfer -- the card driver reported 8 KB moved
+     * per command and 20 MB read for a 10 MB file, still climbing. The card is
+     * not slow and the driver is not stuck; the same data is being fetched
+     * over and over because nothing holds it.
+     *
+     * 2048 blocks is 1 MB, on a machine with 840 MB. The hash grows with it so
+     * a lookup stays short: 512 buckets, four blocks each.
+     *
+     * This is a size, not a policy. If the working set is larger than the
+     * cache the thrashing returns; what changes is that an ordinary file stops
+     * being larger than it.
+     */
+    sb->cache = Cache_CreateCache(glob, 512, 2048, sb->sectorsize, SysBase,
         DOSBase);
     if (sb->cache == NULL)
     {
